WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.730
A liar at least knows what the truth is, and works hard to hide it from you.

2
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:13.220
But there is another kind of person, one who speaks without caring at all about what is true, simply for the sake of having said something.

3
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:19.590
One philosopher considers this second kind more dangerous, because he does not wage war against the truth.

4
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.800
He makes it irrelevant.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.130
A friend asks you about a restaurant that has just opened.

6
00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.820
You have never been there.

7
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.310
But you don't quite feel like saying "I don't know," either.

8
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:49.560
So you recall half a sentence you heard somewhere, add a guess of your own, and speak in a confident voice: "They say the meat is good, but it's a little pricey.

9
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:56.410
Don't go in the evening, it gets crowded." One of the things you said might be true, or all of them might be false.

10
00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:02.300
But here is the real point: while you were talking, whether it was true never concerned you at all.

11
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:08.190
You only wanted to say something, to seem well informed, to keep the conversation from stalling.

12
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:16.640
This little scene captures the very question that led an American philosopher, near the end of the twentieth century, to write his most widely read work.

13
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:23.490
Harry Frankfurt, who taught moral philosophy at Princeton University, published a short essay in nineteen eighty-six.

14
00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:28.740
Its English title contained a slang word considered rather crude in everyday speech.

15
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:37.830
Here, let's just call it "On Nonsense." Some twenty years later, in two thousand five, this slim text was reissued as a small book.

16
00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:44.200
And something unexpected happened: a philosophical essay landed on the bestseller list.

17
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.130
In those pages, people had recognized something they had never been able to name.

18
00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:58.700
Frankfurt's starting point was this: all of us recognize this kind of empty talk, we are exposed to it every day, yet we don't really know what it is.

19
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.710
We confuse it with lying.

20
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:05.840
And yet there is a deep difference between the two.

21
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.050
Think about the liar.

22
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:13.620
To tell a lie, the liar must first know the truth, or at least what he believes to be true.

23
00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.070
Then he says the opposite and steers you in that direction.

24
00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:22.520
The liar, strangely enough, is someone bound to the truth.

25
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:28.090
The truth is his compass; he simply walks in the direction opposite to where the needle points.

26
00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:33.020
In a sense, he takes the truth seriously, because he has to hide it.

27
00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.190
The windbag works differently.

28
00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:40.000
For him, the line between true and false is beside the point.

29
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.370
If what he says turns out to be true, fine; if it turns out to be false, that's fine too.

30
00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:53.060
What concerns him is how his words will make you feel, how they will make him look, what purpose they will serve in the moment.

31
00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:58.630
In Frankfurt's words, the windbag is neither on the side of the truth nor against it.

32
00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:00.840
He never looks at the truth at all.

33
00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.170
He stands somewhere apart from it.

34
00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.820
To illustrate this, Frankfurt turns to an anecdote.

35
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:15.310
A woman named Fania Pascal, who knew Wittgenstein from Cambridge, recounted an incident in a memoir she wrote years later.

36
00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:19.600
She had had her tonsils out and was lying miserably in the hospital.

37
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.290
Wittgenstein came to visit her.

38
00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:32.020
Pascal told him she felt "just like a dog that has been run over." Wittgenstein's face twisted, and he answered sharply: "You don't know what a dog that has been run over feels like."

39
00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:36.630
At first glance, this reaction seems too harsh, even rude.

40
00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:39.640
But Frankfurt catches something important here.

41
00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.770
Pascal was not lying; it was true that she felt awful.

42
00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:51.740
Yet the sentence she chose did not come from any concern to describe what she actually felt, but from a wish to say something striking.

43
00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.590
Her words rested on no knowledge.

44
00:03:54.590 --> 00:04:03.520
What bothered Wittgenstein was not a lie, but precisely this carelessness: the way speech, for a moment, suspended its responsibility to the world.

45
00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.170
The conclusion Frankfurt reaches is unsettling.

46
00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:12.100
In his view, this kind of talk is a greater enemy of the truth than lying is.

47
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:17.670
Because the liar, even when caught in a lie, acknowledges that the truth exists.

48
00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:24.680
The windbag, on the other hand, quietly does away with the very idea that speech should be tied to what is true.

49
00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:31.210
When lies multiply in a society, people grow suspicious, they investigate, they search for the truth.

50
00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:36.780
But when empty talk multiplies, people gradually stop searching for the truth altogether.

51
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.910
They grow used to the idea that nothing really means very much.

52
00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:44.080
This may look like a modern American discovery.

53
00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:50.610
But the many guises of speaking without regard for the truth reach far back, all the way to the streets of Athens.

54
00:04:50.610 --> 00:05:00.820
In the Athens of the fifth century before Christ, a new profession had emerged: teachers who traveled from city to city, teaching young men the art of speaking in exchange for money.

55
00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.190
They were called sophists.

56
00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:10.680
In Athenian democracy, being able to persuade, in the assembly, in the courts, before the people, meant power.

57
00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.170
And the sophists were selling that power.

58
00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:21.020
In Plato's dialogue Gorgias, Socrates comes face to face with the famous orator Gorgias.

59
00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.070
The scene is tense.

60
00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:27.200
Gorgias boasts of how powerful an art oratory is.

61
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:33.890
His brother is a physician, and patients sometimes refuse to take their medicine or to submit to surgery.

62
00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:40.740
Where the physician failed, Gorgias says, I succeeded; I persuaded those patients with my words.

63
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:49.670
He even adds that if a physician and an orator in some city were to debate before the people over which of them should be chosen as the city's doctor, the orator would win.

64
00:05:49.670 --> 00:06:00.360
Socrates pauses here and asks the decisive question: if an orator who knows nothing of medicine can be more persuasive than a physician, then before an audience that does not know,

65
00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.170
the one who does not know is more convincing than the one who does.

66
00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:07.660
Is that an achievement, or a danger?

67
00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:13.710
For Socrates, oratory of this kind is not a true art at all, but a form of flattery.

68
00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:18.320
What cookery is to medicine, this sort of oratory is to justice.

69
00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:23.090
The physician gives the body what is good for it; the cook gives it what is pleasing.

70
00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:26.580
One looks after health, the other after the palate.

71
00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:30.550
This analogy fits Frankfurt's distinction almost exactly.

72
00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:36.760
The problem is not that what is said is false, but that the speaker has no relationship with the truth at all.

73
00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:42.970
The orator does not need to know what he is talking about; it is enough to know what the listener wants to hear.

74
00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:48.380
Frankfurt also offers an observation on why this kind of talk has become so widespread.

75
00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:54.430
When people are obliged to speak about something they know nothing about, empty talk becomes inevitable.

76
00:06:54.430 --> 00:07:03.040
In democratic societies, citizens are expected to have an opinion on everything, from the economy to foreign policy, from vaccines to the climate.

77
00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.370
But none of us can know about all of these things.

78
00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:12.900
The gap between the pressure to hold an opinion and the possibility of holding real knowledge gets filled with hollow verbiage.

79
00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.670
Let us leave the public square of Athens and step into a meeting room of today.

80
00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:21.640
A presentation is cycling across the screen.

81
00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:31.850
Someone says they are proposing "a holistic approach that will create synergy." Someone else talks about "a value-driven transformation journey." Everyone nods.

82
00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:36.140
On the way out, no one can say exactly what was decided.

83
00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.990
Nobody told a lie.

84
00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:41.840
But nobody said anything, either.

85
00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.610
The power of this kind of speech lies in its vagueness.

86
00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:51.060
It cannot be proven false, because it makes no claim.

87
00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:57.590
It cannot be questioned, because whoever questions it is afraid of looking as though they have not understood.

88
00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:03.160
And the speaker pockets the reward of appearing to know something, to be doing something.

89
00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:06.010
Researchers have even tried to measure it.

90
00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:13.340
In twenty fifteen, the Canadian psychologist Gordon Pennycook and his colleagues carried out a curious experiment.

91
00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:20.030
They showed participants sentences produced by a computer stringing together randomly chosen grandiose words.

92
00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:28.000
Sentences like "Hidden meaning transforms unparalleled abstract beauty": grammatically sound, yet saying nothing at all.

93
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.290
Participants were asked to rate how profound these sentences were.

94
00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:37.380
A considerable number of people found deep meaning in these meaningless strings.

95
00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:47.110
The researchers called this tendency "receptivity to pseudo-profound bullshit," and found that it was linked to a habit of accepting one's first intuition without questioning it.

96
00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:51.240
So the success of empty talk does not depend on the speaker alone.

97
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.770
The listener's readiness matters too.

98
00:08:53.770 --> 00:09:00.780
When our ears catch an impressive voice or a clever-sounding word, we are inclined to approve without looking at the substance.

99
00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:05.070
The sophist exists because there is a crowd willing to listen to him.

100
00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:08.080
So what do we have at our disposal as listeners?

101
00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:13.810
In the West, this question has mostly been debated in terms of logic and critical thinking.

102
00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:20.500
But there is another powerful tradition that asks the same question from a very different place: through the ethics of speech.

103
00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:29.270
Sahih Muslim, the hadith collection compiled by Imam Muslim, opens with a lengthy introduction before it turns to the hadiths themselves.

104
00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:36.440
In this introduction there is a short saying attributed to the Prophet: "It is enough of a lie for a person to relate everything he hears."

105
00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:40.890
At first hearing, this sentence may seem strange.

106
00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:45.020
Does someone who passes on what he has heard become a liar?

107
00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:48.030
He did not make it up, after all.

108
00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:51.200
He simply relayed what he heard from someone else.

109
00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:55.650
And this is exactly where the subtlety of the sentence lies.

110
00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:58.820
The saying broadens the definition of a lie.

111
00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:02.790
Knowingly saying something false is a lie, yes.

112
00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:11.240
But spreading everything you hear, without ever asking whether it is true, without filtering it, without weighing it, leads a person to the same place.

113
00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:17.290
Because someone who repeats everything he hears will sooner or later repeat something false as well.

114
00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:23.500
And since he does not concern himself with the truth while doing so, he cannot escape responsibility.

115
00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:29.230
Set this beside Frankfurt's distinction and something striking comes into view.

116
00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:34.480
The Western philosopher says that speech indifferent to truth is something different from a lie.

117
00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.730
This hadith, by contrast, places that very indifference inside the lie.

118
00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:48.500
The two arrive at the same point by different paths: the ethics of speech concerns not only what you say, but how much care you take for the truth as you say it.

119
00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.830
The same introduction records another saying.

120
00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:59.320
Attributed to Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak, the scholar from Khorasan, it reads: "The isnad is part of the religion.

121
00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:10.330
Were it not for the isnad, anyone could say whatever they wished." The isnad is the chain of transmission showing through whom, and to whom, a saying was passed down.

122
00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:15.740
For centuries, hadith scholars in the Islamic world examined these chains.

123
00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:18.590
Did the narrator have a strong memory?

124
00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:21.280
Was he an honest man?

125
00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:24.610
Had he really met the person he claimed to quote?

126
00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:29.220
An entire science was built on asking where a saying came from.

127
00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:35.910
The thinking behind it was simple: a saying has no value unless its source is known, however beautiful it may be.

128
00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:41.000
It is possible to trace this same concern in the Quran as well.

129
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:51.850
In the Surah al-Hujurat, believers are told that when an untrustworthy person brings them news, they should investigate it; otherwise they may unknowingly harm others and come to regret it.

130
00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:59.180
Here, too, responsibility does not end with the one who brings the news; the one who receives it is equally bound to verify it.

131
00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.150
Imam al-Ghazali approaches the matter from a different angle.

132
00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:14.320
In his great work, the Ihya Ulum al-Din, the Revival of the Religious Sciences, he devotes an entire section to the afflictions of the tongue and lists some twenty of them, one by one.

133
00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:19.890
At the top of the list are not the grave sins, such as lying, backbiting, or slander.

134
00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:24.020
At the top of the list is speaking about things that are none of one's concern.

135
00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.190
Next comes saying more than is needed.

136
00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:32.440
For al-Ghazali, the greatest dangers often begin with what seems most innocent.

137
00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:36.250
The habit of idle talk is a letting go of the tongue's reins.

138
00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:41.660
And a tongue whose reins have been let go will, sooner or later, drive a person over a cliff.

139
00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:53.470
Al-Ghazali supports this idea with a hadith transmitted by al-Tirmidhi: "Part of the beauty of a person's Islam is leaving aside what does not concern him." This principle speaks not only to the devout,

140
00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:57.600
but to anyone who wishes to preserve the worth of their words.

141
00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:04.290
Not speaking on matters you do not know, not meddling in affairs that are not yours, lends speech a certain weight.

142
00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:08.260
This awareness left a deep mark on Turkish thought as well.

143
00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:18.950
The Kutadgu Bilig, written by Yusuf Khass Hajib in the eleventh century, reminds the reader again and again that the tongue is a power that can both elevate a person and bring about their ruin;

144
00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:24.520
it regards speaking little, and speaking fittingly, as a sign of wisdom.

145
00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:37.610
Some two centuries later, Yunus Emre expressed the same truth with a simplicity the common people could grasp: A word can end a war, a word can cost a head; a word can turn a poisoned meal into honey and butter.

146
00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.780
In this tradition, speech is a trust.

147
00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:45.070
Every sentence that leaves the mouth must be accounted for.

148
00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:53.040
And that account is not only about whether or not you told a lie; it is also about how much care you took to convey the truth to the one listening.

149
00:13:53.040 --> 00:14:02.130
So what do we see when we consider these words today, in an age when anything we hear can be passed on to thousands of people with the press of a button?

150
00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:05.620
An image arrives on your phone.

151
00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:15.190
A pink sunset, and over it, in elegant lettering, a line: "Come, come, whoever you are, come again.

152
00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:23.800
Whether you are an unbeliever, a fire worshipper, or an idol worshipper, come again." Beneath it, a signature: Rumi.

153
00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:28.570
In Turkey, these lines have become almost synonymous with Rumi himself.

154
00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:34.620
They appear on souvenirs in Konya, on wedding invitations, at the opening of speeches.

155
00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:41.950
And yet scholars who study Rumi have long pointed out that this quatrain appears neither in the Masnavi nor in the Divan-e Shams.

156
00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:49.280
Abdülbaki Gölpınarlı, who brought Rumi's works into Turkish, also noted that these words are not his.

157
00:14:49.280 --> 00:15:00.770
The quatrain is found among poems attributed to Abu Sa'id Abu'l-Khayr, a Sufi mystic from Khorasan who lived before Rumi; but even there, it is difficult to trace it to any definite source.

158
00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:06.660
The saying "Either appear as you are, or be as you appear" shares a similar fate.

159
00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:10.150
It is attributed to Rumi in countless places.

160
00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:15.080
But this sentence, in this form, is nowhere to be found in his works.

161
00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:17.930
No one here means any harm.

162
00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:24.300
Whoever made that image, whoever shared it, whoever liked it, most likely believes they are doing something good.

163
00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:29.230
The sentence is lovely, the message is kind, the name is revered.

164
00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:35.440
But this is exactly what Frankfurt described: no one cares whether the words are true.

165
00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:40.050
What matters is the feeling they create, the prestige they carry.

166
00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:45.620
Rumi's name is used like a seal, and that seal places the sentence beyond question.

167
00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:54.710
And so, across generations, in direct defiance of that warning at the opening of Sahih Muslim, a chain forms of people repeating everything they hear.

168
00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:57.400
This chain has no isnad.

169
00:15:57.400 --> 00:16:03.610
The sad part is this: the more the fabricated sayings multiply, the more the real Rumi fades.

170
00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:10.940
His true voice, so often unsettling, difficult, a voice that disturbs, is lost behind the pink sunsets.

171
00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:22.270
Fewer and fewer people know the verses of the Masnavi that open with the tale of the reed flute, the lament of that reed crying out against separation, that arduous inner journey stretching across thousands of couplets.

172
00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:28.160
Everyone believes they know Rumi, but what they know is very often the things Rumi never said.

173
00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:32.450
This is not something that happens to a single poet alone.

174
00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:42.180
In twenty eighteen, researchers at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology examined news stories that had spread on social media over a period of more than ten years.

175
00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:49.670
They found that false news spread far faster and wider than true news, and that it was roughly seventy percent more likely to be shared.

176
00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:55.240
What's more, those spreading it were mostly not automated accounts, but people.

177
00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:59.530
What was new and surprising proved more appealing than what was true.

178
00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:11.020
Years ago, the software developer Alberto Brandolini put this state of affairs into a half-joking rule: the effort required to refute nonsense is many times greater than the effort needed to produce it.

179
00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:19.150
A fabricated quote can be written in seconds; showing that it is not genuine requires combing through old texts and consulting experts.

180
00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:24.240
Empty talk always has the advantage, because it is never obliged to answer for itself.

181
00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:27.730
Today, a new dimension has been added to this debate.

182
00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:37.140
In twenty twenty-four, three researchers from the University of Glasgow turned directly to Frankfurt in a paper they wrote on large language models.

183
00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:46.550
When these artificial intelligence systems produce a text, they argued, their aim is not to tell the truth, but to find the next word that looks fluent and convincing.

184
00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:52.920
When they produce false information, it is not a lie, because the system has no intention to deceive.

185
00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:56.250
But there is a structural indifference to the truth.

186
00:17:56.250 --> 00:18:03.900
The portrait Frankfurt drew of human beings in nineteen eighty-six is now, forty years later, being debated for machines as well.

187
00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:11.230
This is less a question of whether to trust them than a reminder to us: a fluent sentence is not the same as a true sentence.

188
00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:14.560
Asking for the source matters more than ever.

189
00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:18.050
So what can we do in this age?

190
00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:21.220
Perhaps the answer lies with Rumi himself.

191
00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:26.630
Many of the ghazals in the Divan-e Kabir end with a single word: Khamush.

192
00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:29.800
That is, hush, be silent.

193
00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:33.610
Rumi uses this word almost like a pen name.

194
00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:40.460
A poet who speaks with such passion, and at such length, closes his poems by counseling silence.

195
00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:42.990
There is no contradiction in this.

196
00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:47.440
Because for him, being silent did not mean having nothing to say.

197
00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:51.890
Silence meant standing before something deeper than words themselves.

198
00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:55.540
It meant not forcing words where words fall short.

199
00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:59.190
In everyday life, the equivalent may be very simple.

200
00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:04.760
Being able to say "I don't know, I haven't been there" to a friend who asks about a restaurant.

201
00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:09.210
Not hesitating to ask about a sentence we didn't understand in a meeting.

202
00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:19.420
Pausing for a moment before sharing that beautiful quote that lands on our phone, and asking, "Who really said this?" Admitting that we have no opinion on a subject is not a weakness,

203
00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:22.910
but a stance that protects the value of words.

204
00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:28.160
Frankfurt had said that bullshit wears away the truth more than lying does.

205
00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:33.250
What it wears away is not individual truths, but our very need for the truth.

206
00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:42.340
That short saying at the opening of Sahih Muslim, meanwhile, pulled a person one step back: do not repeat everything you hear.

207
00:19:42.340 --> 00:19:48.870
Between these two voices runs a line stretching from Athens to Konya, from Princeton to the screens of today.

208
00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:57.160
And their shared counsel leads to the same place: to say something, first know it, and if you do not know, be willing to stay silent.

209
00:19:57.160 --> 00:20:00.650
Perhaps this is the hardest virtue of our age.

210
00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:07.340
In a world where speaking is so easy, so cheap and so fast, to remember that we owe an account for every word.

211
00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:13.390
To care not about filling the bag, but about making sure that everything we put into it is true.

212
00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:17.360
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Felsefe.

213
00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:20.850
Until we meet again in a new story.
